Stage 16: ABI v1 open decision 3 priced and found free, decision 13's ceiling
          measured, and the pull family's memory half.

commit:    eb8b1973 (+ this slice)
what:      the three items the finished arms opened. None needed a new ABI; all
           three are answers the branch did not have.

################ decision 3: the rejecting decode policy ################

`ffi/corpus`'s transcode class has 31 `T-dec-*` vectors: malformed UTF-8 in a
string field, which CONTRACT.md says a conformant parser must refuse. Stage 13
established that neither this codec nor `Google.Protobuf` refuses them, so the
decode comparison is like for like and the margin is not bought by skipping
validation. What it did not establish is what refusing would COST.

Built as a third build rather than a runtime flag, for the same reason the floor
is one: a flag would put a branch on both arms' hot path and stop the JIT
devirtualising `Encoding.UTF8`, charging the lossy arm for the strict one's
existence. `/p:AkStrict=true` gives `bin-strict/`, and each build carries the
incumbent as its in-process control so the two are compared THROUGH that control
rather than across sittings.

**It closes all 31 vectors.** The corpus on the strict build:

    transcode         53    22/22       22             22/22         31/31        0
    ...
    1 failure(s) (0 T-dec policy, 1 U-map-entry, 0 other), 0 not run

and `152 checks, 0 failure(s)` on conformance, so nothing else moved.

**And it costs nothing measurable.**

    payload   managed-parse / gp-parse-seq        strict cost
              lossy                strict
    P1.2      0.659 [0.652 0.672]  0.673 [0.629 0.684]   +2.1%
    P2.2      0.726 [0.707 0.739]  0.696 [0.666 0.757]   -4.2%
    P3.1      0.808 [0.774 0.821]  0.837 [0.834 0.871]   +3.6%
    P4.1      0.767 [0.765 0.785]  0.751 [0.741 0.762]   -2.1%

Straddling zero, and the incumbent control -- which neither build changed --
itself moved -4.6% to +1.6% between the two. So the strict cost is inside the
noise floor of the measurement.

**Why it is free, which matters more than the number.** `Encoding.UTF8` already
validates: it has to, in order to know where to substitute U+FFFD. The work is
identical and only the FALLBACK differs, so a rejecting policy is a different
`DecoderFallback` and not a different amount of scanning. **On .NET the argument
against decision 3's rejecting policy cannot be performance.** It is a
behaviour change -- `Google.Protobuf` substitutes and so does every C# consumer
today -- and that is the whole of the case against it here.

################ decision 13: the ceiling on a borrowed span ################

The decode side already hands the host `ak_span` OFFSETS into its own buffer, so
the ABI is ready for a borrowed string view and the facade's `string` is what is
not. Redesigning the facade's public surface around one is a large change with a
lifetime rule attached, so the prize is BOUNDED first: an arm that does
everything the decode does and materialises no string at all. R2's floor-arm
logic, applied to a design question. The gap is the MOST a borrowed view could
ever save.

    payload   pull med ns  no-string med ns  no-string/pull      ceiling
    P1.2          325,732          145,193  0.438 [0.434 0.450]   56%
    P2.2        1,791,784          840,350  0.466 [0.464 0.478]   53%
    P3.1           25,293           14,592  0.582 [0.577 0.588]   42%
    P4.1          265,139          101,456  0.383 [0.370 0.384]   62%

**Forty-two to sixty-two percent of a decode is string materialisation**, and
that is the largest single number this slice has produced. For scale: every
codec difference this slice has measured -- managed against incumbent, push
against pull, core-ffi against managed -- lives inside a band of about 30
percent. The strings are half the decode.

It is a ceiling and not a forecast. A borrowed view still records the offsets, a
consumer that needs a real `string` pays for one anyway, and the facade's
public surface changes. But it says plainly where the remaining money is on
.NET: **decision 13 is a larger lever than any codec choice in this slice, and
larger than decision 3, decision 2 and the push/pull question combined.**

################ the pull family's memory half ################

Stage 14 measured what pull buys (no upcalls, 0.69 to 0.97 of push) and not what
it costs. `ak_bdr_footprint` reports the record buffer's high-water mark:

    payload   wire bytes   pull buffer   ratio
    P1.1             858           576   0.67
    P1.2         218,121       128,136   0.59
    P1.3             605        38,488  63.6
    P2.2         540,422       436,040   0.81
    P2.3         647,024       269,040   0.42
    P2.4         979,465       351,400   0.36
    P3.1          12,097        19,256   1.59
    P4.1          65,321        75,232   1.15
    P5.1-P5.4    116-4.2 MB         72   ~0
    P6.1         123,354       187,232   1.52
    P7.1              98           208   2.12

**0.36 to 1.6 times the wire payload on the real shapes**, which is a real but
bounded trade: pull exchanges the upcalls for a buffer of roughly the payload's
own size, in the host-owned decode context, reused across calls.

Two rows are worth naming. P5.x is ~72 bytes whatever the payload, because a
bulk message has no elements and the record stream carries only the root group.
And **P1.3 is 63x**, because the absent path is 300 elements that each encode to
nothing and the record stream still carries 300 `ak_dfix_ResultRaw` groups --
the same group-size asymmetry decision 9 is about, appearing on the decode side.

################ and the ReadOnlySequence reader, retired with evidence ########

The RPC arm charged the facade arms a flatten: gRPC hands the deserializer a
`ReadOnlySequence` and the facade's `Dec` is over `byte[]`. Stage 15 named a
`Dec` over `ReadOnlySequence` as a real improvement. Instrumented, the question
answers itself in the other direction.

    gRPC delivered 3960 SEGMENTED bodies and 0 single-segment,
    copying 2,140,071,120 bytes in total to flatten them.

So it fires on **every single call** -- the single-segment fast path is never
taken at this payload size. But the copy is 540,422 bytes, and this slice's own
`memcpy floor` arm for P2.2 measures that at **12.8 us** against a CPU per call
of about 4,500 us: **under 0.3 percent**.

**So the item is retired rather than built.** A reader over `ReadOnlySequence`
means every read handling a segment boundary, which risks the single-segment
path that every in-process arm uses, to recover a third of a percent of an RPC.
Measuring the thing before building it is what said so.

################ what is NOT measured here ################

- **Decision 9's sparse fill**, still. It needs an ABI addition and
  `ffi/CLAUDE.md` puts a change to existing behaviour through the aggregating
  session. What is priced is the cost of not having one: 28 to 71 percent of the
  core-ffi encode.
- **Decision 13 itself**, as opposed to its ceiling. The facade's public surface
  and a lifetime rule are the work, and the ceiling is what says whether to do
  it.
- **The strict policy's effect on the ENCODE side.** ABI v1 section 6 already
  settled that UTF-8 is not checked on encode; this build changes decode only.
- **Whether the strict policy changes behaviour for a real ArmoniK consumer.**
  It converts a silently-substituted string into a decode error, which is a
  behaviour change this slice can price and cannot judge.

